 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 515602,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "How many ways are there to choose a hand of 6 cards from a standard deck with the constraint that 3 are <span data-math=\"\\spadesuit\">\\spadesuit</span>, 2 are <span data-math=\"\\heartsuit\">\\heartsuit</span>, and 1 is <span data-math=\"\\clubsuit\">\\clubsuit</span>?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>It does not matter in which order the cards are dealt to you. For each requirement, it is a combination. There are 13 cards in each suit.</p> \n<table class=\"unnumbered\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Requirement</td>\n<td>3 spades</td>\n<td>2 hearts</td>\n<td>1 club</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Formula</td>\n<td>13C3</td>\n<td>13C2</td>\n<td>13C1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Then, use the Multiplication Rule of Counting. The Multiplication Rule of Counting says that if there are <i>n</i> ways to accomplish one task and <i>m</i> ways to accomplish a second task, then there are <span data-math=\"n{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}m\"></span> ways to accomplish both tasks. You can tack on additional tasks by multiplying the number of ways to accomplish those tasks to your previous product.</p>\n<p>The number of ways to have the required hand is <span data-math=\"13{\\text{C}}3{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}13{\\text{C}}2{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}13{\\text{C}}1\"></span>.</p>\n<p>Evaluate each combination separately.</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"13C3 = \\frac{{n!}}{{r!\\left( {n - r} \\right)!}} = \\frac{{13!}}{{3!\\left( {13 - 3} \\right)!}} = \\frac{{13!}}{{3!10!}} = \\frac{{13 \\times 12 \\times 11 \\times \\cancel{{10!}}}}{{3 \\times 2 \\times 1 \\times \\cancel{{10!}}}} = \\frac{{1,716}}{6} = 286\"></span></p>\n<p><span data-math=\"13C2 = \\frac{{n!}}{{r!\\left( {n - r} \\right)!}} = \\frac{{13!}}{{2!\\left( {13 - 2} \\right)!}} = \\frac{{13!}}{{2!11!}} = \\frac{{13 \\times 12\\cancel{{11!}}}}{{2 \\times 1 \\times \\cancel{{11!}}}} = \\frac{{156}}{2} = 78\"></span></p>\n<p><span data-math=\"13C1 = \\frac{{n!}}{{r!\\left( {n - r} \\right)!}} = \\frac{{13!}}{{1!\\left( {13 - 1} \\right)!}} = \\frac{{13!}}{{1!12!}} = \\frac{{13\\cancel{{12!}}}}{{1 \\times \\cancel{{12!}}}} = 13\"></span></p>\n<p>Now you can evaluate <span data-math=\"13{\\text{C}}3{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}13{\\text{C}}2{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}13{\\text{C}}1{\\text{ }} = {\\text{ }}286{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}78{\\text{ }} \\times {\\text{ }}13{\\text{ }} = {\\text{ }}290,004\"></span>.</p>"
    },
